d "082ab1b4-7b0d-5301-97bc-d24a513a580b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549925 #edb/ref 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt[3]{3x+1}+k\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt[3]{3y+1}+k.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt[3]{3y+1}+k \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{3y+1} & =x-k \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{3y+1})}^{3} & ={(x-k)}^{3} \\\\ 3y+1 & =(x-k)^{3} \\\\ 3y & =(x-k)^{3}-1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-k)^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{3}(x-k)^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-k=4\\,⟹\\,k=-4.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549927 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549927 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148747" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549927 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549927 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt[3]{x}}+2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549927 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0b14029b-c258-5cb1-9467-400506e4dea8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549927 #edb/ref 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt[3]{x}}+2\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt[3]{y}}+2.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt[3]{y}}+2 \\\\ x-2 & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt[3]{y}} \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =\\frac{1}{4(x-2)} \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{y})^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{4(x-2)})}^{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{64(x-2)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{64(x-2)^{3}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne 0,\\,f(x)\\ne 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne 2,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ne 0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549934 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549934 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148431" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549934 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549934 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}-4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549934 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "11b34dc5-01d5-59cc-afad-bce1d288e367" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549934 #edb/ref 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt[3]{x}-4\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt[3]{y}-4.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt[3]{y}-4 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =x+4 \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{y})^{3} & =(x+4)^{3} \\\\ y & =(x+4)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=(x+4)^{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549936 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549936 #edb/ref 1069 "q-277826" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549936 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549936 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[3]{2x-1}}+2.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549936 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "14c0067f-8b9b-586e-a9fe-ecef26e510a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549936 #edb/ref 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[3]{2x-1}}+2\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[3]{2y-1}}+2.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt[3]{2y-1}}+2 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{2y-1} & =\\frac{3}{x-2} \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{2y-1})^{3} & ={(\\frac{3}{x-2})}^{3} \\\\ 2y-1 & =\\frac{27}{(x-2)^{3}} \\\\ 2y & =\\frac{27}{(x-2)^{3}}+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{27}{2(x-2)^{3}}+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{27}{2(x-2)^{3}}+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{1}{2},\\,f(x)\\ne 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ne 2,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ne \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549939 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549939 #edb/ref 1069 "q-277054" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549939 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549939 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=5\\sqrt[3]{x}+3.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549939 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1883917d-29b4-5836-94bf-f6ba9fe053f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549939 #edb/ref 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=5\\sqrt[3]{x}+3\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=5\\sqrt[3]{y}+3.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\sqrt[3]{y}+3 \\\\ 5\\sqrt[3]{y} & =x-3 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =\\frac{1}{5}(x-3) \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{y})^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{5}(x-3))}^{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{125}(x-3)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{125}(x-3)^{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549944 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549944 #edb/ref 1069 "q-277956" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549944 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549944 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $f(x),$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{k}{\\sqrt{8x+3}}+2,\\,x>-\\frac{3}{8}\n$$\n\nhas an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9}{2(x-2)^{2}}-\\frac{3}{8},\\,x>2.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$ Assume that $k>0.$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549944 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "22e0c565-a427-5cb0-889a-a9c4e5867def" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549944 #edb/ref 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{k}{\\sqrt{8x+3}}+2\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{k}{\\sqrt{8y+3}}+2.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{k}{\\sqrt{8y+3}}+2 \\\\ x-2 & =\\frac{k}{\\sqrt{8y+3}} \\\\ \\sqrt{8y+3} & =\\frac{k}{x-2} \\\\ {(\\sqrt{8y+3})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{k}{x-2})}^{2} \\\\ 8y+3 & =\\frac{{k}^{2}}{(x-2)^{2}} \\\\ 8y & =\\frac{{k}^{2}}{(x-2)^{2}}-3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{{k}^{2}}{8(x-2)^{2}}-\\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{k}^{2}}{8(x-2)^{2}}-\\frac{3}{8},\\,x>2.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{k}^{2}}{8}=\\frac{9}{2}\\,⟹\\,k=6.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549947 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549947 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148496" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549947 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549947 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt[3]{x-3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549947 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "286413cf-e05b-52a9-9010-9fd10625bdfe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549947 #edb/ref 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt[3]{x-3}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt[3]{y-3}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt[3]{y-3} \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y-3} & =3x \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{y-3})}^{3} & ={(3x)}^{3} \\\\ y-3 & =27{x}^{3} \\\\ y & =27{x}^{3}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=27{x}^{3}+3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549953 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549953 #edb/ref 1069 "q-1485