is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{36(2-x)^{2}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx>0,\\,f(x)<2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx<2,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)>0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549965 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549965 #edb/ref 1069 "q-277959" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549965 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549965 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $f(x),$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2}{k\\sqrt[3]{6x-1}}+2,\\,x\\ne \\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nhas an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4}{81(x-2)^{3}}+\\frac{1}{6},\\,x\\ne 2.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549965 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5cb5bb60-d097-50b4-9ba5-bde88e86a4d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549965 #edb/ref 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{k\\sqrt[3]{6x-1}}+2\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2}{k\\sqrt[3]{6y-1}}+2.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{k\\sqrt[3]{6y-1}}+2 \\\\ \\frac{2}{k\\sqrt[3]{6y-1}} & =x-2 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{6y-1} & =\\frac{2}{k(x-2)} \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{6y-1})}^{3} & ={(\\frac{2}{k(x-2)})}^{3} \\\\ 6y-1 & =\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{k}^{3}(x-2)^{3}} \\\\ 6y & =\\frac{8}{{k}^{3}(x-2)^{3}}+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{3{k}^{3}(x-2)^{3}}+\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{4}{3{k}^{3}(x-2)^{3}}+\\frac{1}{6},\\,x\\ne 2.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{3{k}^{3}}=\\frac{4}{81}\\,⟹\\,k=3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549966 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549966 #edb/ref 1069 "q-277840" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549966 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549966 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt{x-5}+6$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=16(x-6)^{2}+5.$ What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549966 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "635cbae5-da30-5f6f-818b-8857829587ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549966 #edb/ref 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt{x-5}+6\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt{y-5}+6.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt{y-5}+6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{k}\\sqrt{y-5} & =x-6 \\\\ \\sqrt{y-5} & =k(x-6) \\\\ {(\\sqrt{y-5})}^{2} & ={(k(x-6))}^{2} \\\\ y-5 & ={k}^{2}(x-6)^{2} \\\\ y & ={k}^{2}(x-6)^{2}+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)={k}^{2}(x-6)^{2}+5.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{k}^{2}=16\\,⟹\\,k=4.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ge 5,\\,f(x)\\ge 6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ge 6,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ge 5.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549974 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549974 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148550" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549974 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549974 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=2-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3x+2}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549974 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7e395659-7f2e-5dda-a6c4-f51ac9defcd5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549974 #edb/ref 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=2-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3x+2}}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=2-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3y+2}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3y+2}} \\\\ x-2 & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3y+2}} \\\\ 2-x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3y+2}} \\\\ \\sqrt{3y+2} & =\\frac{1}{2-x} \\\\ {(\\sqrt{3y+2})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2-x})}^{2} \\\\ 3y+2 & =\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}} \\\\ 3y & =\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3(x-2)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{3(x-2)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx>-\\frac{2}{3},\\,f(x)<2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx<2,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)>-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549988 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549988 #edb/ref 1069 "q-277045" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549988 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549988 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=6-4\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549988 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aa587d06-3d76-54dc-bc14-c7a25e414d3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549988 #edb/ref 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=6-4\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=6-4\\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6-4\\sqrt{y} \\\\ 4\\sqrt{y} & =6-x \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =\\frac{1}{4}(6-x) \\\\ (\\sqrt{y})^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{4}(6-x))}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{16}(6-x)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{16}(6-x)^{2}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0,\\,f(x)\\le 6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx\\le 6,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)\\ge 0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550013 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550013 #edb/ref 1069 "q-270776" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550013 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #ed